Consider a heterostructure formed by stacking a two-dimensional van der Waals ferromagnetic layer (FM) on a two-dimensional van der Waals antiferromagnetic layer (AFM). When such an FM/AFM bilayer is field-cooled below the Néel temperature of the AFM, the hysteresis loop of the FM is generally shifted along the magnetic-field axis; this shift is the exchange-bias (EB) effect, characterized by a bias field H_EB and an enhanced coercivity. In a layered van der Waals AFM, "interlayer spin coupling" refers to the magnetic exchange interaction between adjacent magnetic monolayers across the van der Waals gap, which determines the AFM stacking order (e.g., A-type interlayer antiferromagnetism) and the orientation of uncompensated interfacial moments presented to the FM.

Determine, quantitatively and mechanistically, the role played by this interlayer spin coupling in establishing the exchange-bias effect in FM/AFM van der Waals heterostructures. Specifically:
(a) derive how the sign, magnitude, and blocking temperature of H_EB depend on the strength and sign of the interlayer exchange J_⊥ within the AFM (and at the FM/AFM interface);
(b) identify the conditions under which interlayer coupling produces compensated vs. uncompensated interfacial spin configurations and the resulting EB behavior;
(c) explain how thickness (number of AFM layers, parity effects), stacking order, and external tuning (pressure, gating, strain) of J_⊥ modify H_EB and the coercivity enhancement.

